\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces HealthGAN (WGAN-GP) architecture and training configuration.}}{26}{table.caption.34}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Fidelity of the three retained HealthGAN synthetic draws against the real training partition (lower is better on both measures). The spread across draws is negligible. Draw $0$ was selected as representative.}}{27}{table.caption.35}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces PATE-GAN architecture, training, and privacy configuration}}{30}{table.caption.36}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Differences of the DP pipeline from the non-DP Pythia pipeline (\S \ref {sec:methods:pythia}). All other logic is shared.}}{34}{table.caption.37}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Comparative summary of the five synthesisers. All generators are trained on the same $57{,}214$-record training partition of the Diabetes 130-US Hospitals benchmark and supply synthetic outputs whose sizes are matched to the partitions used in the downstream evaluation pipeline of Chapter~\ref {ch:results}. Privacy budgets are reported as target $(\varepsilon , \delta )$ values; the realised $\varepsilon $ for Pythia-1B-DP is reported in \S \ref {sec:results:rq3:llm-toggle}.}}{34}{table.caption.38}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Coverage of the evaluation framework for RQ1. Each instrument captures a different utility or privacy dimension and has a blind spot significantthat requires complementary metrics or diagnostics.}}{47}{table.caption.61}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Mean Kolmogorov-Smirnov statistic and mean pairwise correlation difference per generator (lower is better on both). KS is averaged over all compared columns; PCD is the mean absolute difference between the real and synthetic correlation matrices. The best non-trivial value in each column is shown in bold.}}{51}{table.caption.66}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Train-on-Synthetic, Test-on-Real (TSTR) utility for all five generators and the Train-on-Real (TRTR) reference, evaluated on the real held-out split ($n = 14{,}304$) with \emph {readmitted} as the target. The best synthetic AUC-ROC per classifier block is shown in bold.}}{55}{table.caption.74}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Cox proportional-hazards confidence-interval overlap per generator: the number of covariates whose synthetic $95\%$ hazard-ratio interval overlaps the real interval, out of those fitted (higher is better). HealthGAN is fitted on twelve covariates because one zero-variance column was dropped.}}{57}{table.caption.80}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces TSTR utility under the discarded three-bin age encoding versus the retained ten-bin encoding. Test set: real held-out split ($n = 14{,}304$); target: \emph {readmitted}; classifier: Gradient Boosting. $\Delta $ is computed as the 10-bin value minus the 3-bin value.}}{58}{table.caption.81}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Median and fifth-percentile nearest-neighbour distances, with the median distance ratio, per generator. The fifth-percentile synthetic-to-real distance is the disclosure-relevant tail, that is, the closest synthetic records to the real data. The real-to-real median nearest-neighbour distance is $2.019$ for reference. Larger synthetic-to-real distances and $\text {NNDR} \geq 1$ are consistent with lower median proximity risk only when the generator also remains close to the real-data support.}}{59}{table.caption.82}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Nearest-neighbour adversarial accuracy per generator. Train and test adversarial accuracy near $0.5$ indicates good resemblance; privacy loss (test $-$ train) near $0.0$ indicates no memorisation.}}{60}{table.caption.83}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.8}{\ignorespaces Membership-inference attack results per generator. AUC near $0.5$ and advantage near $0.0$ indicate that the adversary cannot distinguish training members from non-members.}}{60}{table.caption.84}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.9}{\ignorespaces Attribute-inference advantage per generator and attribute (positive values indicate leakage above the marginal baseline). The final column is the per-generator maximum advantage.}}{61}{table.caption.85}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.10}{\ignorespaces Outlier versus non-outlier linkability per generator (percentage of real records with a synthetic neighbour within radius $2.636$). Low outlier linkability indicates that the generator does not concentrate mass around vulnerable records; a positive gap indicates coverage of common rather than extreme patterns. The gap is reported in percentage points.}}{61}{table.caption.86}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.11}{\ignorespaces Comprehensive summary of the twelve headline metrics across the five generators and the real (TRTR) reference. Arrows indicate the preferred direction; ($\approx 0.5$) indicates that values closest to $0.5$ are best, and ($\approx 0$) indicates negligible leakage. The nearest-neighbour distance and ratio rows are two-sided: values near the real-data spacing ($\approx 2.02$ and $\approx 1$, respectively) are the privacy-neutral reference, values below signal proximity risk, and larger values are protective only while the generator remains on the real-data support. Bold marks the best synthetic value on each utility row. PATE-GAN's nearest-neighbour distance and ratio are not treated as protective because they reflect off-manifold output and should be read with Section~\ref {sec:results:rq2:privacy}.}}{63}{table.caption.88}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.12}{\ignorespaces Within-family differential-privacy transition for the GAN family (HealthGAN $\to $ PATE-GAN). $\Delta $ is the private value minus the non-private value; on a utility row a negative $\Delta $ denotes a loss.}}{69}{table.caption.93}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.13}{\ignorespaces Within-family differential-privacy transition for the LLM family (Pythia-1B $\to $ Pythia-1B-DP), a clean toggle on a shared backbone at achieved $\varepsilon = 4.998$. $\Delta $ is the private value minus the non-private value.}}{70}{table.caption.94}%
